Three method classes behave differently as the targets move away from the sources (Table~\ref{tab:primary}, Figure~\ref{fig:ratio}).
\emph{Model-based predictors} that estimate the target from a model fitted on the sources (IRT model, kNN, ridge) halve the error of uniform sampling at $n\le25$ when targets are interpolated ($0.55$ and $0.65$ for the IRT model at $n=10$ and $25$), but their advantage shrinks as $n$ grows (IRT model $0.78$, kNN $1.12$, ridge $0.73$ at $n=100$).
For targets above every source, the IRT model and kNN are worse than uniform on every design (IRT model $1.96$ $[1.82,2.04]$ on a uniform design and $1.94$ on a balanced one; kNN $2.42$ and $2.10$), rising to about $3.8$ for the IRT model at $n=400$, and ridge is worse on a uniform design ($1.47$).
Ridge on a balanced design is the exception ($0.88$ as a model, $0.84$ as a plug-in), and is the model-based representative chosen by leave-one-benchmark-out in extrapolation; we did not test why it extrapolates better than the logistic predictors.
\emph{Anchor and information-selected subsets} do not beat uniform at $n=100$ in interpolation or lineage ($0.95$ and $1.03$ for anchors with IRT plug-in) and lose heavily in extrapolation ($1.83$); deterministic information-top selection with the sample mean is $2.99$ in extrapolation and above $3$ in the other two splits.
\emph{Methods that keep a probability design or correct for the residual} (stratified or balanced sampling, calibrated regression, the difference estimator) are below $1$ in every split, except calibrated regression on a uniform design in extrapolation ($1.04$).

